>>> notes identity --project demo 
qargi uses no Codex hook to record the lead identity, so start the session with `QARGI_LEAD=ID` set: `feature`, `local`, `review` and `release` read it; only marshal verbs take `--as ID`. Without it the anonymous user@host works but only warns.
exit 0
>>> notes message --project demo 
To send a Codex session a message without attaching to it, run `codex queue --thread ID_OR_NAME --message TEXT`, adding `--resume` to resume the thread after queueing and `--remote unix://` for a remote server. One thread is open in one client at a time; another client reads it or forks it. A message is data for that session, never an approval of a plan, a permission or a question.
exit 0
>>> notes session-model --project demo 
A Codex session's own model is chosen in Codex (`codex -m MODEL` or its configuration), not by qargi; qargi chooses only the model of a member it starts with `qargi local run`.
exit 0
>>> notes skills-dir --project demo 
Codex loads a checkout's skills from `.agents/skills` and the user's from `~/.codex/skills` (`CODEX_HOME/skills`) and `~/.agents/skills` (a directory with a SKILL.md each; a linked directory works); `install.sh --user --runtime codex` links qargi's skills into `~/.codex/skills`. To hide a skill without removing it, add an entry to `skills.config` in `~/.codex/config.toml` that names it with `enabled = false`; qargi only reads that file. Managed member snapshots default off. With the owner gate, canonical skills reaching the actual role are copied into private CODEX_HOME/skills; this destination declaration does not qualify loading or change capability dates. Committed checkout skills and readable files retain independent discovery/access behavior; prose grants no permissions.
exit 0
>>> notes spawn --project demo 
Prepare with `qargi local prepare` (--role, optional --profile, task block), then `qargi local run RUN`. Prepare privately retains requested model and effort; omitted model and effort options reuse them. Different --model or non-null native --effort controls require supported overrides, unverified for Codex; choose another profile and reprepare. Requests do not confirm observed identity; bound runtime/mode/catalog/profile/policy drift requires reprepare. The member runs `codex exec` in its clone, prompt via stdin, under its class's sandbox; JSON events: OUT/transcript, last message: OUT/report.md. A Codex member runs commands only inside its sandbox. `--dry-run` prints the command without changing bytes.
